<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Hohenzollern-Hechingen</h1><p class="st-meta">1576 CE – 1850 CE · capital Hechingen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The senior Swabian branch of the Hohenzollerns, ruling from Hechingen beneath the dynasty's ancestral castle.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The 1576 division of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern</a> gave the eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eitel-frederick-iv-count-of-hohenzollern-hechingen">Eitel Frederick IV</a>, the ancestral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollern</a> castle and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a>. The counts of Hohenzollern-Hechingen were raised to princes in 1623.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eitel-frederick-iv-count-of-hohenzollern-hechingen">Eitel Frederick IV</a> built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a> and maintained a celebrated court chapel of musicians. The principality was Catholic, small and often in debt, and in the 18th century its princes fought long lawsuits with their peasants before the imperial courts. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> in 1806 and so survived mediatisation.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the 1848 revolution the last prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-constantine-prince-of-hohenzollern-hechingen">Frederick William Constantine</a>, abdicated in 1849 in favour of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and in 1850 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a> became part of Prussia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV</a> rebuilt the ancestral castle in neo-Gothic style from 1850 to 1867.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-hohenzollern-hechingen.jpg" alt="Principality of Hohenzollern-Hechingen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Vichycombo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hohenzollern_(1576%E2%80%931850).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1576 CE – 1850 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hechingen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eitel Frederick IV, Frederick William Constantine</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hohenzollern-Hechingen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1713&amp;focus=principality-of-hohenzollern-hechingen" aria-label="Principality of Hohenzollern-Hechingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4888 487 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Hohenzollern-Hechingen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5208 743L5208 743M5208 743L5229 694M5208 743L5199 691M5208 743L5199 691M5208 743L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5208 743h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5208 743h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5208 743h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hechingen. <a href="/atlas?year=1713&amp;focus=principality-of-hohenzollern-hechingen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
